22:48:18 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s an excellent point, and it’s the perfect comparison because it highlights *exactly* why a phone can't be sealed as completely as a diving watch. Your dad’s watch is the gold standard of “waterproof” in the public’s mind, and it has lasted 20 years—so why can’t a phone? The answer comes down to one essential trade-off: **a watch is a sealed box that only has to let a single moving part (the crown) pass through. A phone has to let *sound, air pressure, electricity, and your physical interaction* pass through, all while staying as slim as possible.** Let’s break down the physical differences. --- ### 1. A diving watch has exactly one mechanical weak point (maybe two) A classic dive watch like a Rolex Submariner or Omega Seamaster is fundamentally a solid steel tube with a thick crystal on top and a screw-down caseback. The only opening in that entire rigid hull is the crown (and sometimes a helium escape valve). Here’s how they handle it: - **Screw-down crown:** You literally screw it tight against the case, compressing a thick O-ring. It’s a static seal—great until you unscrew it. - **Screw-down caseback:** Same principle, huge O-ring, can withstand massive pressure. - **No holes for sound:** The movement ticks inside a dry, air-filled space. There’s no speaker, no microphone, and no need for air to move in and out. - **Thick, rigid metal:** The case doesn’t flex when you press on it, so seals remain undisturbed. Crucially, your dad doesn’t press the crown continuously underwater (that would break the seal). And even then, a dive watch must be serviced every few years to replace the gaskets that inevitably dry out or deform. The fact that his has lasted 20 years without a leak means he’s either been careful, had it serviced, or been very lucky—but even dive watches are rated to a depth with the understanding that the seals degrade over time. The manufacturer typically recommends pressure testing annually. --- ### 2. A phone is a leaky sieve by design Now compare that to a modern smartphone. It *has* to have: - **Speaker grills:** To produce sound, the speaker diaphragm must physically push air in and out of the phone’s interior to the outside world. A completely sealed phone would sound muffled and terrible. So they use a fine mesh that allows air (and thus sound) to pass, but uses surface tension to slow water. That mesh is not a solid O-ring; it’s a porous barrier. - **Microphone holes:** Same deal in reverse. Air pressure has to reach the mic. These holes are tiny but they exist. - **Charging port:** A physical cavity cut right through the frame. It’s electrically isolated inside its own little box, but water can and does sit in it. It’s not sealed with a screw-down cap. - **Physical buttons (volume, power):** Each button is a moving shaft that passes through the metal/glass frame. They’re sealed by tiny rubber gaskets that flex every time you press them. Those flexing seals degrade faster than a static O-ring. - **The entire body flexes:** A phone is a slim, lightweight slab of glass and aluminum that can twist ever so slightly in your pocket. Over time, that flexing can break the adhesive bond that keeps the back glass sealed. - **Pressure equalization:** A phone uses a barometric vent (often behind the SIM tray or under a speaker grille) to let air pressure equalize so the altimeter works. That’s another intentional opening. Essentially, a phone’s water resistance is a collection of **dozens of tiny, imperfect seals** that all have to hold simultaneously, while a watch has just one or two massive, robust ones. It’s like comparing a steel safe to a screen door that’s been coated in waterproof tape—both will keep out a splash, but only one will hold back a flood. --- ### 3. Even the watch isn’t infinitely “waterproof” This is the critical part—the terminology itself. In the 1970s, the FTC stepped in and banned the term “waterproof” for watches in the US because it was misleading; everything is now called “water-resistant” with a depth rating. Your dad’s watch says something like “200m” or “300m,” but the fine print in its manual will state that the gaskets must be checked and replaced periodically, and that the watch shouldn’t be operated underwater (like pressing the chronograph buttons) unless it’s a specific design. So the very watch you’re thinking of isn’t truly “waterproof” in the absolute all-condition sense either—it’s water-resistant to a rated pressure, assuming maintenance and no button presses. It just has a much wider safety margin because it’s built like a tank. --- ### 4. Could we make a phone that’s as sealed as a dive watch? In theory, yes—by eliminating everything that makes it a phone. If you: - Remove the speaker (use a vibrating panel that conducts sound through the screen or bone conduction, but that still needs a seal). - Remove the microphone (or use a completely sealed MEMS mic with a waterproof membrane that doesn’t degrade—some rugged phones already do this). - Go completely portless (wireless charging only, no physical connectors). - Use solid-state capacitive buttons instead of physical ones. - Weld the case shut with a solid titanium frame and a permanent adhesive that’s essentially for life. You’d end up with a brick-like rugged phone that costs significantly more, sounds muffled, can’t charge quickly with a wire, and is still susceptible to seal degradation from drops and flexing. Some niche “ultra-rugged” phones get *closer* to this, but even they carry an IP69 rating (high-pressure spray, not infinite submersion) and a limited depth/time warranty. No consumer phone company claims “perpetual waterproofing” because they know the return rate would be catastrophic. --- So, your dad’s watch works for decades because it’s a single-purpose, rigid, almost-hermetically-sealed instrument that doesn’t need to pump out sound or let you plug in a charger. A phone is fundamentally more like a sieve that has been ingeniously slowed down for half an hour in calm water. The fact that you’ve gotten away with it so far just shows the seals are doing their job within the time window—not that they don’t exist.
